      "heading": "Side Effects, Precautions, and Warnings for Blood Sugar",
      "body": "<p>Transitioning to a lower‑carb, higher‑fiber diet can trigger a brief “low‑carb adjustment” phase. During this period, the body shifts from relying on glucose to using ketones and fatty acids for energy, which may manifest as fatigue, mild hunger, or mood swings. These symptoms are typically self‑limited, resolving within a few days to a week as insulin sensitivity improves and glycogen stores are depleted. Monitoring blood glucose every 2–3 days during the first month helps distinguish normal adaptation from hypoglycemia, especially for individuals on insulin or sulfonylureas.</p>\n<p><strong>Side effects that warrant medical attention</strong> include persistent dizziness, extreme fatigue, or unexplained weight loss. Such signs may indicate hypoglycemia, over‑reduction of carbohydrate intake, or an underlying condition such as adrenal insufficiency. If these symptoms occur, contact your healthcare provider promptly and consider a temporary increase in carbohydrate intake or a dose adjustment of glucose‑lowering medications.</p>\n<p>Maintaining a detailed log of blood sugar readings, medication doses, and dietary intake is essential. Sharing this log during appointments allows clinicians to fine‑tune medication regimens, reducing the risk of hypoglycemia or hyperglycemia.</p>\n<p><strong>Medications That May Need Monitoring</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table class=\"md-table\"><thead><tr><th class=\"tbl-th\">Medication Class</th><th class=\"tbl-th\">Guidance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=\"tbl-row-even\"><td class=\"tbl-td\">Insulin</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">Consult your prescribing doctor before making any changes — do not adjust this medication yourself</td></tr><tr class=\"tbl-row-odd\"><td class=\"tbl-td\">Sulfonylureas</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">Consult your prescribing doctor before making any changes — do not adjust this medication yourself</td></tr><tr class=\"tbl-row-even\"><td class=\"tbl-td\">ACE inhibitors</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">Consult your prescribing doctor before making any changes — do not adjust this medication yourself</td></tr><tr class=\"tbl-row-odd\"><td class=\"tbl-td\">Metformin</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">Consult your prescribing doctor before making any changes — do not adjust this medication yourself</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"tbl-source\">Source: Baragetti et al., 2023</p>\n<p>The table underscores that insulin and sulfonylureas directly influence glucose levels and therefore require close supervision when carbohydrate intake is altered. ACE inhibitors and metformin, while not directly affecting glucose, can influence renal function and electrolyte balance; changes in diet may affect their pharmacokinetics, so professional guidance remains essential.</p>\n<p><strong>Practical context for patients</strong>:</p>\n<ul><li>Start with a gradual reduction of refined carbs (e.g., 10–15 g per day) to allow the body to adapt.</li><li>Increase soluble fiber (oats, legumes, berries) to promote satiety and stabilize post‑prandial glucose.</li><li>Keep a consistent meal schedule; skipping meals can precipitate hypoglycemia in insulin‑treated patients.</li><li>Hydrate adequately; dehydration can concentrate glucose and affect drug absorption.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Action tip</strong>: Talk to a registered dietitian before making major dietary changes. A dietitian can design a meal plan that balances macronutrients, ensures adequate micronutrient intake, and aligns with your medication schedule, thereby minimizing the risk of adverse events while maximizing glycemic control.</p>\n",
